# noramedi-attachment-vps2.env.example — F4-ATTACH-001-R1
#
# Template for /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/backup.env — the credentials/
# config file the three scripts.noramedi-attachment-vps2-{backup,check,
# restore-proof}.sh systemd units load via EnvironmentFile=-. NOT installed
# by this task. Copy, fill in the real values on VPS1 ONLY, then:
#   install -o root -g noramedi-attach-backup -m 0640 <filled-in copy> \
#           /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/backup.env
#
# ⚠ NEVER commit a filled-in copy of this file. NEVER paste a filled-in
# value into ClickUp, chat, a commit message, or this repository. The
# secret-bearing artifacts (RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE's *contents*, the private
# SSH key, and this env file itself) must exist only as files on VPS1 under
# /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/ — see the credential-layout section below —
# plus a SEPARATE off-host escrow copy per the discovery document §4, the
# same escrow discipline already applied to pgBackRest's repo2-cipher-pass.
#
# ── credential layout: a DEDICATED, NON-HOME directory ─────────────────────
# The noramedi-attach-backup OS account is created --no-create-home and every
# systemd unit sets ProtectHome=yes, so nothing may live under
# /home/noramedi-attach-backup (which does not exist). Every credential this
# task needs — the env file, the restic passphrase, the SSH private key, its
# known_hosts pin, and an SSH client config that ties them together — lives
# instead under /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/, which ProtectSystem=strict
# leaves readable by default (this account only ever needs to READ it):
#
#   /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/                    0750 root:noramedi-attach-backup
#   /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/backup.env           0640 root:noramedi-attach-backup  (this file, filled in)
#   /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/restic-password      0600 noramedi-attach-backup:noramedi-attach-backup
#   /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/id_ed25519_attachment_vps2       0600 noramedi-attach-backup:noramedi-attach-backup  (private key)
#   /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/id_ed25519_attachment_vps2.pub   0644 noramedi-attach-backup:noramedi-attach-backup  (public half; also installed on VPS2)
#   /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/known_hosts          0644 noramedi-attach-backup:noramedi-attach-backup  (pinned via ssh-keyscan — see below)
#   /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/.ssh/config          0600 noramedi-attach-backup:noramedi-attach-backup  (see the SSH transport section)
#
# Every one of the three systemd units additionally sets
# `Environment=HOME=/etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2` (see each unit's own
# comment) so that the OS `ssh` client restic's sftp backend shells out to
# resolves `$HOME/.ssh/config` to this dedicated file automatically — no
# restic command-line change is needed, and ProtectHome=yes does not
# interfere with this because it governs the real /home, /root and
# /run/user/* paths, never wherever `$HOME` happens to be pointed.

# ── restic repository (restic's own variables — read directly by the
#    `restic` binary; this env file is the ONLY place they are ever written) ──

# SFTP transport to a DEDICATED, restricted account on VPS2 — NOT the
# pgBackRest repo2 account, NOT any GlitchTip account, NOT a DICOM/imaging
# credential. See the discovery document §4a for the full VPS2-side sshd
# chroot/path contract (ForceCommand internal-sftp, ChrootDirectory, no
# shell, `from=<VPS1-IP>` source restriction on the authorized_keys entry).
#
# ⚠ PATH IS RELATIVE TO THE CHROOT, NOT THE HOST FILESYSTEM. If sshd's
# ChrootDirectory for this account is `/srv` (§4a's recommended shape), the
# repository directory on the HOST is `/srv/noramedi-attachment-vps2/repo`,
# but the path an SFTP CLIENT (hence restic) must use is relative to `/srv`
# as the chroot's new `/` — i.e. `/noramedi-attachment-vps2/repo`, NOT
# `/srv/noramedi-attachment-vps2/repo` (that would resolve, post-chroot, to
# the nonexistent host path `/srv/srv/noramedi-attachment-vps2/repo`). Adjust
# this to match whatever ChrootDirectory is actually provisioned — the two
# MUST agree, and this file cannot verify that for you.
RESTIC_REPOSITORY=sftp:noramedi-attach-backup@<VPS2_HOST>:/noramedi-attachment-vps2/repo

# Absolute path to a file on VPS1 (mode 0600, owner noramedi-attach-backup)
# containing ONLY the repository passphrase, no trailing newline required
# (restic tolerates one). This passphrase is generated once at `restic init`
# time and MUST be escrowed off both VPS1 and VPS2 before production
# activation — see §4. It is never derived from, or shared with, any other
# credential in this program (pgBackRest cipher-pass, JWT secrets, DB
# passwords, GlitchTip DSN, DICOM credentials all remain fully independent).
RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE=/etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/restic-password

# restic's on-disk metadata cache. A dedicated directory, NOT under
# noramedi-attach-backup's $HOME (the account is --no-create-home, and $HOME
# is separately repointed at /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2 for SSH config
# resolution only — see below; the restic cache itself stays under
# /var/lib/noramedi-attachment-vps2 so the systemd units' ReadWritePaths can
# be minimal and explicit).
RESTIC_CACHE_DIR=/var/lib/noramedi-attachment-vps2/cache

# ── SSH transport (consumed by restic's sftp backend via the OS ssh client,
#    NOT a restic-native variable) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# restic's sftp backend has no per-repository "which key" flag of its own; it
# simply execs the OS `ssh` binary, which resolves its config the normal
# OpenSSH way. Every systemd unit sets `Environment=HOME=/etc/noramedi/
# attachment-vps2` (see each unit file), so `ssh` resolves
# `$HOME/.ssh/config` to the dedicated file below WITHOUT any restic
# command-line change and WITHOUT ever touching a real home directory:
#
#   # /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/.ssh/config  (mode 0600, owned by
#   # noramedi-attach-backup; the .ssh directory itself mode 0700 — OpenSSH
#   # refuses a config/key it considers group/world-writable)
#   Host <VPS2_HOST>
#       IdentityFile /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/id_ed25519_attachment_vps2
#       IdentitiesOnly yes
#       UserKnownHostsFile /etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/known_hosts
#       StrictHostKeyChecking yes
#       BatchMode yes
#
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_SSH_KEY_PATH=/etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/id_ed25519_attachment_vps2
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS_PATH=/etc/noramedi/attachment-vps2/known_hosts
# (documented here for the operator's reference only — no script in this
# repository reads either variable; the effective binding is the ssh config
# file above, resolved via $HOME.)
#
# StrictHostKeyChecking=yes + BatchMode=yes together mean: an unrecognized
# or changed host key, or any other condition that would normally prompt
# interactively, makes `ssh` (and therefore restic) fail closed instead of
# either hanging on a prompt no one can answer or silently trusting an
# unverified host — never set StrictHostKeyChecking=no.
#
# ⚠ Known program gotcha (see docs/program/phases/F4_STORAGE_AND_BACKUP.md,
# F4-2-R1's "libssh2 negotiates ecdsa-sha2-nistp256, not ssh-ed25519" finding):
# that finding is specific to pgBackRest's bundled libssh2 and does not
# automatically apply to restic (which uses the OS `ssh` binary, i.e.
# OpenSSH's own negotiation) — but it is exactly the class of surprise the
# host-key verification step above exists to catch BEFORE activation, not
# after. Verify with `ssh-keyscan` against the actual VPS2 host and pin the
# result in the known_hosts file above before the first real run.

# ── this script's own variables (scripts/noramedi-attachment-vps2-backup.sh
#    and -check.sh / -restore-proof.sh headers document each in full) ──
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_SOURCE_DIR=/var/www/noramedi/server/uploads
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_CMD_TIMEOUT=3600
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_CHECK_SUBSET=5%
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_STATUS_FILE=/var/lib/noramedi-attachment-vps2/status/attachment-vps2-status.json
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_STATUS_LOCK_FILE=/var/lock/noramedi-attachment-vps2-status.lock
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_RESTOREPROOF_LOCK_FILE=/var/lock/noramedi-attachment-vps2-restore-proof.lock

# ── monitoring (Healthchecks.io-style dead-man's-switch heartbeats — SAME
#    provider/pattern this program already uses for every other job, see
#    scripts/noramedi-opscheck.sh's header. Each of the three jobs pings a
#    SEPARATE check so a dashboard can distinguish "no backup ran" from
#    "backups are running but were never integrity-checked" from "restore has
#    never been proven". Use NEW checks — do not reuse the DB backup's or
#    pgBackRest's existing check URLs.) ──
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_PING_URL=
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_CHECK_PING_URL=
# NORAMEDI_ATTACHMENT_VPS2_RESTOREPROOF_PING_URL=
